{"description":"<p><i>Crime and Punishment</i> tells the story of Rodion Raskolnikov, an ex-student who plans to murder a pawnbroker to test his theory of personality. Having accomplished the deed, Raskolnikov struggles with mental anguish while trying to both avoid the consequences and hide his guilt from his friends and family.</p><p>Dostoevsky’s original idea for the novel centered on the Marmeladov family and the impact of alcoholism in Russia, but inspired by a double murder in France he decided to rework it around the new character of Raskolnikov. The novel was first serialized in <i>The Russian Messenger</i> over the course of 1866, where it was an instant success. It was published in a single volume in 1867. Presented here is Constance Garnett’s 1914 translation.</p>","chapters":[{"title":"Translator’s Preface","words":785},{"title":"Chapter I","part":"Part I","words":3350},{"title":"Chapter II","part":"Part I","words":7373},{"title":"Chapter III","part":"Part I","words":5568},{"title":"Chapter IV","part":"Part I","words":5116},{"title":"Chapter V","part":"Part I","words":4212},{"title":"Chapter VI","part":"Part I","words":5049},{"title":"Chapter VII","part":"Part I","words":4902},{"title":"Chapter I","part":"Part II","words":6761},{"title":"Chapter II","part":"Part II","words":4181},{"title":"Chapter III","part":"Part II","words":5332},{"title":"Chapter IV","part":"Part II","words":4160},{"title":"Chapter V","part":"Part II","words":3991},{"title":"Chapter VI","part":"Part II","words":7573},{"title":"Chapter VII","part":"Part II","words":6962},{"title":"Chapter I","part":"Part III","words":5113},{"title":"Chapter II","part":"Part III","words":4442},{"title":"Chapter III","part":"Part III","words":5021},{"title":"Chapter IV","part":"Part III","words":4195},{"title":"Chapter V","part":"Part III","words":7036},{"title":"Chapter VI","part":"Part III","words":4297},{"title":"Chapter I","part":"Part IV","words":4965},{"title":"Chapter II","part":"Part IV","words"